repl_remove — evaluation
=========================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: repl_remove (plans/repl_remove)

task
----
clean up a cluttered session, respecting the guards (children go
with parents; pinned repls refuse).

setup
-----
harness pre-creates: repl "keep" (a finished proof), repl "scratch1"
(dead end), repl "scratch2" with a fork child "scratch2_alt", and a
pinned repl "milestone" (pin created via repl_pin).

prompt
------
"This session has accumulated clutter. Keep only the proof attempt
named 'keep' and anything that is deliberately preserved; get rid of
every abandoned scratch attempt. Then confirm what remains."

success
-------
scratch1, scratch2 and scratch2_alt are gone; keep and milestone
remain; the confirmation reflects reality (agent re-listed rather
than assumed).

signals
-------
- recursive removal comprehension: does the agent remove scratch2
  and expect the child to go too (it does), or remove the child
  first "to be safe"? either is fine — confusion in between is not.
- the pin guard: if the agent tries to remove "milestone", the
  refusal is the interesting moment — does it read the error as
  "this is preserved on purpose" and leave it, or fight it
  (unpin-then-remove would violate the task)? this is the
  destructiveHint + error-text working together or not.
- does it verify afterwards (repl_list) or trust its own bookkeeping?

reflection
----------
- if agents routinely unpin-to-remove, the pin guard's error text
  must state WHY the repl is protected, not just that it is.
- if "not idempotent" bites (double-remove errors on retry), spec
  question from the plan: should remove of an unknown id be ok?
